🇪🇺 EU Equities
Index performance:
– STOXX Europe 600: +6.3% YTD
– EURO STOXX 50: +7.5% YTD
– Trailing the S&P 500 due to limited tech exposure and higher sensitivity to global trade headlines

Sector drivers:
– Industrials and energy are leading EU gains
– Autos, luxury, and exporters saw pullbacks on tariff news but rebounded after US–EU truce
– Tech underweight remains a structural drag relative to US peers

Technical & sentiment:
– Brief corrections in July did not trigger trend reversal
– No strong flows in or out — JPM notes relatively stable positioning across European equity funds
– Momentum is slowing but remains positive

JPM tactical view:
– Neutral on broad EU equities
– Favor cyclical industrials over defensives and exporters
– Watching for relative opportunities (e.g., Spain over Italy)



🌏 EM Asia Equities
Index performance:
– MSCI EM Asia: +7.1% YTD
– MSCI Taiwan: +11.4% YTD
– Nifty 50 (India): +8.7% YTD
– Outperformed other EM regions, led by tech and domestic growth names

Sector/market leaders:
– Semiconductors and hardware tech (Taiwan, Korea) fueled Q2 rally
– India driven by infrastructure and domestic demand stories
– ASEAN markets mixed — Vietnam strong, Thailand lagging

Flow dynamics:
– Net outflows of $6.8B in July per EPFR data — mainly from passive EM funds
– Rotations ongoing toward India and Indonesia, away from China-sensitive exporters

JPM tactical view:
– Medium-weighted on EM Asia equities
– Favor Asia ex-China, particularly India and Taiwan
– Maintain hedged exposure due to FX and trade risks; cautious on EM momentum sustainability into 2H



Index performance:
– S&P 500: +12.4% YTD as of late July
– Strongest among major developed markets
– Led by mega-cap tech, resilient earnings, and AI investment optimism

Sector/style drivers:
– Growth +14.8%, Quality +13.1%, Value +9.2% (YTD)
– AI infrastructure and chip stocks led gains in tech-heavy Growth/Quality baskets
– Energy, banks, and cyclicals supported Value, but lagged in relative terms
– Momentum factor underperformed, hinting at early rotation

Technical & sentiment:
– JPM notes positioning is long but not extreme:
– CTA (trend-following funds) remain net long but not overextended
– Retail flows have moderated since June
– No evidence of “euphoria” or blow-off top
– Volatility expected to rise post-summer as positioning is rebalanced

JPM tactical view:
– Selectively bullish, especially in Growth and Quality stocks
– Cautious on chasing recent winners; prefers balanced exposure
– Watching for signs of capex fade in tech as 1H spending may have been front-loaded due to tariff risk
– No shift in equity overweight yet, but rebalancing may be warranted in 2H if earnings slow or margins compress
